<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">
    <!-- The gym reminders are scheduled alarms on this device, and this lets the
         notification plugin lay them down again after a reboot, which is
         otherwise where every pending one goes to die. They are the only
         scheduled notifications left — the end of a rest is posted when it
         happens, see below.

         **This app declares no alarm permission, and that is Play policy rather
         than a technical choice.** Read this before adding one back:

           USE_EXACT_ALARM        Play grants it only to apps whose core function
                                  is an alarm clock or a calendar. A tracker with
                                  a rest timer in it does not qualify, and an app
                                  declaring it is refused publication. No amount
                                  of justification changes that — unlike the
                                  foreground service below, there is no review to
                                  win.
           SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM   unrestricted, but denied by default from
                                  Android 14, so it costs a trip to a system
                                  settings toggle the user has to find.

         So nothing is handed to Android in advance at all: the app rings the end
         of a rest itself, at the moment its own countdown reaches zero, and the
         foreground service below is what keeps it alive to do that. An inexact
         alarm was the alternative and it lands late, which for a rest timer is
         its own kind of broken. See lib/services/rest_alarm.dart and issue #70.

         **The foreground service, unlike the alarm, is winnable.** Every
         FOREGROUND_SERVICE_* type needs a Play Console declaration and a video
         demonstrating the feature, and the requirement is per declared type, so
         there is no cheaper type to retreat to. It is declared and justified
         rather than avoided: see RELEASING.md for the text submitted and the
         video that goes with it. The service is what keeps the live session's
         isolate alive with the phone in a pocket, which is what makes Done on a
         notification button reach the workout it is about. -->

    <!-- **What is here is not the whole list.** Plugins merge their own in, and
         the merged result is what Play publishes and what the phone's app info
         shows. Read it, rather than this file, before answering "what does this
         app ask for":

             ./gradlew -q processReleaseMainManifest
             grep uses-permission \
               build/app/intermediates/merged_manifest/release/*/AndroidManifest.xml

         Today that adds WAKE_LOCK (flutter_foreground_task and media3 both), a
         legacy WRITE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE and CAMERA (camera_android_camerax).
         CAMERA is asked for and used; the other two are capped at old Android
         versions or invisible, and are left alone. Everything a plugin wanted
         that this app does not do is removed below. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED"/>
    <!-- Android 13+ will not show any notification without it being granted,
         including the live workout and the workout reminders. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS"/>
    <!-- The rest-finished notification buzzes: it makes no sound of its own —
         the app plays the ding itself, so that the rest-alert volume can reach
         it — and a phone in a gym bag is not always somewhere a ding carries.
         See lib/services/rest_alarm.dart. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.VIBRATE"/>
    <!-- The live workout runs as a foreground service, which is what keeps the
         session's isolate alive while the phone is in a pocket — see
         lib/services/workout_shade.dart. Declared with a type, as Android 14 and
         up require, and justified at Play review rather than avoided:

           specialUse   health is the nearest established type and needs a sensor
                        permission (ACTIVITY_RECOGNITION or BODY_SENSORS) this app
                        has no business asking for, since it reads no sensors.
                        shortService caps at three minutes and a workout is not
                        three minutes. dataSync describes the wrong thing and is
                        capped at six hours a day. So the subtype below is spelled
                        out in words, which is what specialUse asks for. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE"/>
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_SPECIAL_USE"/>

    <!-- **Taken back out of the merged manifest.**
         Three permissions no line above asks for, merged in by plugins that
         declare everything they *could* do. What a phone lists is the merged
         result, so removing them here is the only way the list matches the app.
         `tools:node="remove"` is the merger's own instruction and needs the
         xmlns:tools declaration on <manifest> above.

         **Check each of these still has nothing behind it before deleting a
         line.** A feature that starts recording audio or reading shared storage
         would be refused at runtime with the permission removed, and the error
         does not name this file.

           RECORD_AUDIO           camera_android_camerax declares it whether or
                                  not a recording asks for audio. Both cameras
                                  here pass enableAudio: false — see
                                  lib/services/set_video_recorder.dart and
                                  lib/screens/scan_screen.dart. Left in, it shows
                                  as "Microphone" on the Play listing and in app
                                  info, which is a claim this app cannot make.
           ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE   androidx.media3-common, behind video_player,
                                  which queries connectivity for streaming. This
                                  app plays local files only and holds no INTERNET
                                  permission in a release build at all.
           READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE  not declared by any plugin: the manifest merger
                                  adds it implicitly beside camerax's
                                  WRITE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE, and unlike that one it
                                  arrives with no maxSdkVersion cap. Nothing here
                                  reads shared storage; clips live in the app's
                                  own private directory. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECORD_AUDIO"
        tools:node="remove"/>
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE"
        tools:node="remove"/>
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE"
        tools:node="remove"/>

    <application
        android:label="Foss Lift"
        android:name="${applicationName}"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher">
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:launchMode="singleTop"
            android:taskAffinity=""
            android:theme="@style/LaunchTheme"
            android:configChanges="orientation|keyboardHidden|keyboard|screenSize|smallestScreenSize|locale|layoutDirection|fontScale|screenLayout|density|uiMode"
            android:hardwareAccelerated="true"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize">
            <!-- Specifies an Android theme to apply to this Activity as soon as
                 the Android process has started. This theme is visible to the user
                 while the Flutter UI initializes. After that, this theme continues
                 to determine the Window background behind the Flutter UI. -->
            <meta-data
              android:name="io.flutter.embedding.android.NormalTheme"
              android:resource="@style/NormalTheme"
              />
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER"/>
            </intent-filter>
            <!-- Shared things arrive as fosslift://<host>/<code>, which is what
                 a shared link and a scanned QR both contain: theme/FLT1.… for a
                 colour theme, routine/FLR1.… for a routine. **Every host the
                 app shares needs a filter here**, or the link is inert outside
                 the app however well routeForLink handles it — routines were
                 shareable for a while with no filter to catch them.

                 A custom scheme rather than an https App Link on purpose: it
                 needs no domain, no hosting and no network, so it cannot stop
                 working when nobody is paying for a server. An https filter can
                 be added alongside this one later without invalidating any code
                 already shared — the payload after the prefix is identical. -->
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.BROWSABLE"/>
                <data android:scheme="fosslift" android:host="theme"/>
                <data android:scheme="fosslift" android:host="routine"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>
        <!-- The live-workout foreground service. The name is the plugin's and
             must not change.

             The subtype string below is what a Play reviewer reads first, so it
             says what the service does in the user's terms rather than in
             Android's. Keep it in step with the declaration in RELEASING.md and
             with what the demo video actually shows — a reviewer comparing the
             three is the point of the exercise. -->
        <service
            android:name="com.pravera.flutter_foreground_task.service.ForegroundService"
            android:foregroundServiceType="specialUse"
            android:exported="false">
            <property
                android:name="android.app.PROPERTY_SPECIAL_USE_FGS_SUBTYPE"
                android:value="Keeps a workout in progress on screen in the notification shade, with its rest timer, while the phone is put away."/>
        </service>
        <!-- What carries a press on one of the live workout's buttons. Android
             delivers it as a broadcast, and the plugin starts an isolate of its
             own to run the Dart callback if the app is no longer alive — which is
             the case this exists for, since nothing holds the process open during
             a workout. Without this receiver the buttons are drawn and do
             nothing. The name is the plugin's and must not change. -->
        <receiver
            android:exported="false"
            android:name="com.dexterous.flutterlocalnotifications.ActionBroadcastReceiver" />
        <!-- The notification plugin's other receivers: one to show a scheduled
             reminder or rest ding when its alarm fires, one to re-schedule
             everything after a reboot or an app update. -->

        <receiver
            android:exported="false"
            android:name="com.dexterous.flutterlocalnotifications.ScheduledNotificationReceiver" />
        <receiver
            android:exported="false"
            android:name="com.dexterous.flutterlocalnotifications.ScheduledNotificationBootReceiver">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.BOOT_COMPLETED"/>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MY_PACKAGE_REPLACED"/>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.QUICKBOOT_POWERON" />
                <action android:name="com.htc.intent.action.QUICKBOOT_POWERON"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </receiver>
        <!-- The status-bar icon for the live workout. flutter_foreground_task
             cannot be handed a drawable name: it looks a meta-data entry up in
             the merged manifest by the name Dart gave it and uses the resource
             id it holds. So this is the other half of WorkoutShade._iconMetaData
             — keep the two spellings in step. Every other notification takes the
             same drawable through the notification plugin's initialize, in
             lib/services/notifications.dart. -->
        <meta-data
            android:name="com.fosslift.foss_lift.NOTIFICATION_ICON"
            android:resource="@drawable/ic_stat_fosslift" />
        <!-- Don't delete the meta-data below.
             This is used by the Flutter tool to generate GeneratedPluginRegistrant.java -->
        <meta-data
            android:name="flutterEmbedding"
            android:value="2" />
    </application>
    <!-- Required to query activities that can process text, see:
         https://developer.android.com/training/package-visibility and
         https://developer.android.com/reference/android/content/Intent#ACTION_PROCESS_TEXT.

         In particular, this is used by the Flutter engine in io.flutter.plugin.text.ProcessTextPlugin. -->
    <queries>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.PROCESS_TEXT"/>
            <data android:mimeType="text/plain"/>
        </intent>
        <!-- Android 11+ hides installed apps from us unless we say what we
             intend to hand off to. Without this the demo-link button cannot
             see that a browser exists and the launch is simply refused. -->
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW"/>
            <data android:scheme="https"/>
        </intent>
    </queries>
</manifest>
